# Universe content · planets (rocky, watery and small worlds)
# researched: Wikipedia "List of planet types"; The Planetary Society "The Different Kinds of Exoplanets";
#   Britannica "super-Earth"; EarthSky (super-Earth vs mini-Neptune vs sub-Neptune)
# Gas and ice giants: content/gas-giants.unt. Physics of interiors and air: content/physics.unt.
# Spec: docs/CONTENT-FORMAT.md · wiki/systems/r06-planet.md

# ── By size ──

@planet sub-earth
name: Sub-Earth
real: yes
says: much smaller and lighter than Earth
example: Mercury, Mars

@planet earth-size
name: Earth-sized rocky planet
real: yes
says: rock and metal, about Earth's size
example: Earth, Venus

@planet super-earth
name: Super-Earth
real: yes
mass-earth: 1..10
says: rocky, bigger than Earth but well below Neptune; the most common kind found so far

@planet mega-earth
name: Mega-Earth
real: yes
says: a rocky planet far heavier than a super-Earth

@planet mini-neptune
name: Mini-Neptune (gas dwarf)
real: yes
mass-earth: up to about 10
says: a small planet wrapped in a thick hydrogen-helium air; where super-Earths end and mini-Neptunes begin is unknown

# ── By makeup ──

@planet rocky
name: Rocky planet
real: yes
says: a stone mantle over a metal core

@planet iron
name: Iron planet
real: yes
says: mostly metal core with little rock

@planet carbon
name: Carbon planet
real: hypothetical
says: rich in carbon instead of oxygen; diamond and graphite instead of ordinary rock

@planet coreless
name: Coreless planet
real: hypothetical
says: rock all the way through, with no metal core

@planet helium
name: Helium planet
real: hypothetical
says: a world whose air has lost its hydrogen, leaving helium

@planet ocean
name: Ocean planet
real: yes
says: liquid-water oceans hundreds of kilometers deep, with no dry land
air: nitrogen 70%, oxygen 18%, water vapour 10%, argon 1%, carbon dioxide 1%, neon 0.002%, helium 0.0005%, krypton 0.0001%, xenon 0.00001%  (thick, wet air)

@planet hycean
name: Hycean world
real: hypothetical
says: a mini-Neptune whose mass is mostly a warm water ocean under a hydrogen-helium air

@planet desert
name: Desert planet
real: yes
says: dry rock and sand, little or no surface water
air: carbon dioxide 95%, nitrogen 2.8%, argon 2%, oxygen 0.2%, carbon monoxide 0.06%, neon 0.00025%, krypton 0.00003%, xenon 0.000008%  (thin, like Mars)

@planet snowball
name: Snowball planet
real: yes
says: a world whose oceans have frozen over, far from its star
air: nitrogen 95%, methane 4%, carbon monoxide 1%  (cold, like Titan or Pluto)

@planet ice-world
name: Ice world
real: yes
says: ice from pole to pole, often with water below

# ── By heat and orbit ──

@planet lava
name: Lava planet
real: yes
says: so close to its star that its surface is molten rock
example: Kepler-10b, CoRoT-7b
air: rock vapour: sodium, potassium, silicon monoxide, oxygen  (a thin haze of boiled rock)

@planet chthonian
name: Chthonian planet
real: hypothetical
says: the bare core left after a giant planet's gas was stripped away by its star

@planet greenhouse-world
name: Greenhouse world
real: yes
says: thick air trapping heat until the surface bakes
example: Venus

@planet temperate
name: Temperate planet
real: yes
says: in the habitable zone, where liquid water can last
example: Earth
air: nitrogen 78%, oxygen 21%, argon 0.9%, carbon dioxide 0.04%, neon 0.0018%, helium 0.0005%, methane 0.0002%, krypton 0.0001%, xenon 0.00001%, water vapour (varies)  (like Earth: the noble gases at their real shares)

@planet tidally-locked
name: Tidally locked planet
real: yes
says: one side always faces its star; one side burns, one side freezes, with a twilight ring between
air: nitrogen 80%, carbon dioxide 15%, oxygen 4%, argon 1%  (winds carry heat to the night side)

@planet eccentric
name: Eccentric-orbit planet
real: yes
says: a stretched orbit that swings close to its star and far away, making wild seasons

@planet circumbinary
name: Circumbinary planet
real: yes
says: orbits two stars at once

@planet rogue
name: Rogue planet
real: yes
says: no star at all, drifting through space, warmed only from inside
mix: rogue
makeup: rock: silicate crust, iron core; its air frozen onto the ground as ice: nitrogen, water, carbon dioxide; warmed only from inside
density: 5.0
albedo: 0.5

# ── Earth-family examples ──

@example mercury
name: Mercury
real: yes
type: sub-earth
note: smallest planet, closest to our own star, almost no air, has a magnetic field

@example venus
name: Venus
real: yes
type: greenhouse-world
note: about 96% carbon dioxide air, about 460 °C at the surface, no magnetic field

@example earth
name: Earth
real: yes
type: temperate
note: plate tectonics, a magnetic field, oceans and life

@example mars
name: Mars
real: yes
type: sub-earth
note: thin carbon dioxide air, polar ice caps, no global magnetic field

# ── Generator rules ──

@rule planet-seed
real: rule
says: every planet is generated live from the world seed; nothing is pre-built

@rule planet-size-air
real: rule
says: bigger, colder planets keep more air; small hot ones lose it (content/physics.unt)

@rule planet-zone
real: rule
says: where a planet forms (inside or outside the frost line, in or out of the habitable zone) sets its makeup and temperature

# ── Layers: what lies under the ground, from the surface down to the middle ──
# from-r, to-r: where the layer lies, as shares of the world's radius from its middle (1 the surface); in: the kinds of
#   ground that have it (content/terrain.unt @terrain ids); matter: rock, metal (molten in an outer core), ice, water;
#   mix: what it is made of (content/minerals.unt @mix; ground: the world's own surface mix); temp-k, density-g-cc and
#   pressure-gpa run from the layer's top to its bottom. Earth's layers are the Preliminary Reference Earth Model
#   (Dziewonski and Anderson 1981): crust to about 35 km, upper mantle to 660 km, lower mantle to 2,891 km, liquid outer
#   core to 5,150 km, solid inner core to the middle at 6,371 km. The Moon's from Apollo seismology and GRAIL (crust about
#   40 km, an iron core about 350 km across); an icy moon's from Galileo's gravity and magnetic readings at Europa (an ice
#   shell some tens of km over an ocean about 100 km deep, over rock and an iron core). A world of another size keeps the
#   shares; its pressures scale with its surface gravity times its radius (the card works them out).
@layer crust
name: Crust
from-r: 0.995
to-r: 1
matter: rock
mix: ground
temp-k: 288..900
density-g-cc: 2.7..2.9
pressure-gpa: 0..1
real: yes
says: the thin skin the ground is made of: the world's own surface rock, half a percent of its radius
in: default, temperate, ocean, desert, lava, snowball, tidally-locked

@layer upper-mantle
name: Upper mantle
from-r: 0.896
to-r: 0.995
matter: rock
mix: upper-mantle
temp-k: 900..1900
density-g-cc: 3.3..4.4
pressure-gpa: 1..24
real: yes
says: hot solid rock that creeps a few centimetres a year, carrying the plates; its top melts into the magma of volcanoes
in: default, temperate, ocean, desert, lava, snowball, tidally-locked

@layer lower-mantle
name: Lower mantle
from-r: 0.546
to-r: 0.896
matter: rock
mix: lower-mantle
temp-k: 1900..4000
density-g-cc: 4.4..5.6
pressure-gpa: 24..136
real: yes
says: rock crushed into denser crystals, most of the world's volume; it churns slowly, taking the core's heat up
in: default, temperate, ocean, desert, lava, snowball, tidally-locked

@layer outer-core
name: Outer core
from-r: 0.19
to-r: 0.546
matter: metal
mix: metal-core
temp-k: 4000..5400
density-g-cc: 9.9..12.2
pressure-gpa: 136..330
real: yes
says: molten iron and nickel, stirred by the heat below and the world's turn; its flow makes the magnetic field
in: default, temperate, ocean, desert, lava, snowball, tidally-locked

@layer inner-core
name: Inner core
from-r: 0
to-r: 0.19
matter: metal
mix: metal-core
temp-k: 5400..5700
density-g-cc: 12.8..13.1
pressure-gpa: 330..364
real: yes
says: solid iron and nickel, as hot as a star's face but held solid by the pressure; it grows as the world cools
in: default, temperate, ocean, desert, lava, snowball, tidally-locked

@layer moon-crust
name: Crust
from-r: 0.977
to-r: 1
matter: rock
mix: ground
temp-k: 250..500
density-g-cc: 2.9..3.0
pressure-gpa: 0..0.2
real: yes
says: the old cooled rock of a magma ocean, thicker on the far side
in: moon

@layer moon-mantle
name: Mantle
from-r: 0.2
to-r: 0.977
matter: rock
mix: upper-mantle
temp-k: 500..1700
density-g-cc: 3.3..3.4
pressure-gpa: 0.2..4.5
real: yes
says: cold rigid rock; the deep moonquakes start near its bottom
in: moon

@layer moon-core
name: Core
from-r: 0
to-r: 0.2
matter: metal
mix: metal-core
temp-k: 1700..1900
density-g-cc: 7..7.5
pressure-gpa: 4.5..5
real: yes
says: a small iron core, partly molten round a solid middle
in: moon

@layer icy-shell
name: Ice shell
from-r: 0.985
to-r: 1
matter: ice
mix: icy-shell
temp-k: 100..270
density-g-cc: 0.92..0.93
pressure-gpa: 0..0.03
real: yes
says: a shell of ice tens of kilometres thick, cracked by the tides
in: icy-moon

@layer hidden-ocean
name: Hidden ocean
from-r: 0.9
to-r: 0.985
matter: water
mix: sea
temp-k: 270..275
density-g-cc: 1.0..1.05
pressure-gpa: 0.03..0.13
real: yes
says: salt water, kept liquid by the tides' heating, over the rock below
in: icy-moon

@layer icy-moon-mantle
name: Rock mantle
from-r: 0.35
to-r: 0.9
matter: rock
mix: rock-mantle
temp-k: 275..1300
density-g-cc: 3.0..3.6
pressure-gpa: 0.13..2.5
real: yes
says: rock, perhaps with sea-floor vents where the water meets it
in: icy-moon

@layer icy-moon-core
name: Core
from-r: 0
to-r: 0.35
matter: metal
mix: metal-core
temp-k: 1300..1600
density-g-cc: 5.5..8
pressure-gpa: 2.5..3.5
real: yes
says: iron and iron sulfide
in: icy-moon

@rule planet-interior
real: rule
says: every rocky planet gets core, mantle and crust; heat decides tectonics or a stagnant lid, volcanoes, and a magnetic field

@rule planet-conditions
real: rule
says: every planet is rated on the 10 world conditions (temperature, gravity, air pressure, air makeup, radiation, water, day length, surface, magnetic shield, local life)

@rule planet-hypothetical
real: rule
says: hypothetical planet types can be generated but are rare, and they're marked as such
